<content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<div class="os-raise-familysupport">
  <para>Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ad776e2f4b03a08bf69ddddc59a1efd31551b1a0">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>
</div>
<para>In an exponential function, the output is multiplied by the same factor every time the input increases by one. The multiplier is called the growth factor.</para>
<para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Refer back to your work in the table in activity 5.4.3: Exponential Change: The Growth Factor, where </para>
<para>\(n=500 \cdot 2^t\) and \(p=100 \cdot 3^t\). Use that information and the given coordinate planes to graph the following using the Desmos tool below. Students were provided access to Desmos.</para>
<para>Use the equation \(n=500 \cdot 2^t\) to answer questions 1–7</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
<!--Q#1-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the domain? </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((-\infty, +\infty)\) or \(-\infty &lt; t &lt; +\infty\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#2-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> What is the range? </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((0, +\infty)\) or \(0 &lt; n &lt; +\infty\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#3-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Write the answer as an inequality. </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(n \geq 500 \cdot 2^t\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#4-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Graph \((t,n)\) when \(t\) is 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <media><image alt="A discrete graph." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/37a00581b3abd08d729d2ba1239f30105cc37689"/></media></solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#5-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> On the graph of \(n\), where can you see each number that appears in the equation? </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>500 is the vertical intercept (or the value of \(n\) when \(t\) is 0). The 2 is the doubling of the \(n\) value every time \(t\) increases by 1.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#6-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Graph \((t,p)\) when \(t\) is 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4. (If you get stuck, you can create a table.) </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <media><image alt="A discrete graph." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7fba9b9732a9cb1c08657bac7e1b245f83eb1f88"/></media></solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<!--Q#7-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> On the graph of \(p\), where can you see each number that appears in the equation? </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>100 is the vertical intercept (or the value of \(p\) when \(t\) is 0). The 3 is the tripling of the \(p\) value every time \(t\) increases by 1</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<title>Video: Graphing Exponential Functions</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about graphing exponential functions.</para>
